# EMBERDEEP — The three endings and the closing card.

=== ending_oath
!music Ending
@wren: Thirty years. That's what the ledger says a strong heart buys.
@wren: Thirty years for Maelis to write the truth down. For Hollowmere to find another way, with its eyes open this time.
@brannoc: Lass. No.
@wren: Somebody has to, Brannoc. And I'm the only one down here who's any good with fire.
@pip: Lantern-friend, no. A grove with no you in it is only a garden.
@wren: Then plant one with a lantern in it. You're the tender. Tenders don't sleep till the tending's done.
@maelis: I'll write it all. Every name. Yours on the first page, in my best hand. No footnotes.
@wren: One footnote. Say I was scared, and I did it anyway.
@narrator: Ilsa comes down the last stair. There is nothing left behind the door to hold. She sees the Flame, and her sister's face, and understands.
@ilsa: Wren. No. That was meant to be me.
@wren: It was meant to be nobody. But it's me, and I'm choosing it. That's the difference.
@narrator: Wren puts Old Tom into Ilsa's hands.
@wren: Somebody has to light the lanterns. Chapel Row first. Not too early, or it's out by midnight.
@ilsa: I know. I taught you that.
@wren: Then follow the light home. Like you promised.
!flash
!sfx Light
@narrator: The lamplighter steps into the Flame. It does not hurt as much as she feared. Mostly, it is warm.
@narrator: At the Well's edge the old man closes his eyes, and the cold settles back into its long sleep. It is not rest. It is only quiet.
!fade
!music Town
@narrator: Hollowmere, many years later.
@narrator: The Lantern Order is gone. In its hall Maelis Thorne keeps a library anyone may read, and above its door is carved every Warden's name. The last is the newest, and the brightest.
@narrator: Dagna and Brannoc argue in the forge every morning. They have been married eleven years. They are still arguing about the wedding.
@narrator: Pip tends a glowroot grove in the temple garden, where the children of Hollowmere are forbidden to pick "the cousins".
@narrator: And every evening at dusk, Ilsa Varn carries a battered lantern named Old Tom down Chapel Row and lights the lanterns for her sister. A tall young lamplighter named Tobin walks beside her. He still does the walk.
@tobin: Chapel Row first?
@ilsa: Always Chapel Row first.
@narrator: In a few more years, Hollowmere will have to choose again. This time everyone will know what they are choosing. Maelis has made very sure of that.
@narrator: And far below, one lantern burns at the bottom of the world. The lamplighter who keeps it is not afraid of the dark any more. She is the part of it that's lit.
!ending oath

=== ending_dark
!music Ending
@wren: No. No more hearts in that fire. Not mine, not Ilsa's, not anybody's.
@wren: Three hundred years we've fed it people, and it's still hungry. It will always be hungry.
@aurelian: Then let it go out. Please.
@narrator: Wren does not reach for the Flame. She watches it gutter and shrink and, with a sound like a held breath let go, go out.
!sfx Frost
!shake
@narrator: The old man smiles, and comes apart like snow in a warm hand. But grief outlives the one who grieves. The cold stays. With nothing to hold it, it rises.
@maelis: It's coming up. All of it. We have minutes. Possibly fewer.
@brannoc: Up. Now. Nobody stops.
@narrator: They run. At the last door Ilsa is waiting, with nothing left to hold, so she takes her sister's hand instead, and they climb.
!fade
@narrator: They burst out of the Vaultgate at dawn with the cold at their heels, and Wren climbs the temple tower and rings the bell until her arms give out.
@narrator: Hollowmere goes down the mountain that morning: carts, cradles, looms, Bess's great iron stewpot, Fen's entire shop on one very patient mule.
@narrator: Old Hesta refuses to be carried. Brannoc carries her anyway. She hums the whole way down and hits him with her stick at every switchback.
@narrator: Captain Rennick is the last one out. He closes the gate behind him, for form's sake.
@narrator: Behind them the Pale fills the valley like milk poured into a bowl. The lanterns of Hollowmere go out one by one, and do not come back.
!fade
!music TownSorrow
@narrator: The lowland town of Brackenford, that autumn. It is warm here. It is not home.
@narrator: Maelis has written everything down and sent copies to every city that will read them, and several that won't. The Pale is coming, slowly. Now, at least, the world knows what it is.
@narrator: Dagna has set up a forge in a borrowed barn. Brannoc works the bellows. Neither of them mentions that he hasn't gone back to his lodgings in a month.
@narrator: Pip has planted glowroots in a crack in the town wall. The townsfolk call them weeds. Pip calls the townsfolk rude, but very quietly.
@ilsa: Do you regret it?
@wren: Every night. And every morning I'm glad. I think that's how it'll be now.
@tobin: Wren! It's dusk! Can I do Chapel Row?
@wren: There isn't a Chapel Row here, Tobin.
@tobin: Then I'll name one.
@narrator: That night the people of Hollowmere light lanterns in a borrowed town. Not for the Wardens, and not for the dead. For each other.
@narrator: Somewhere far behind them the cold is still coming. But no one will ever again be sent down the stair alone.
!ending dark

=== ending_dawn
!music Story
@wren: No. There's another way. Ilsa said there had to be, and she was right.
@narrator: Wren kneels at the Well's edge and opens her pack. Twelve Memory Shards lie inside, glowing softly, warm as hands.
@wren: These are yours. I've been carrying them for you. I'm sorry I looked. But someone should know she was here.
@narrator: She puts the first in the old man's palm. A snowstorm. A candle. A baby's furious first cry.
@aurelian: Lira. Her name was Lira.
@narrator: Then the next, and the next: a bed under the eaves, paper lanterns in a window, grey water, a funeral in the snow, a long dark stair. All of it. Every piece.
@narrator: The last shard is the smallest. Wren folds his fingers over it. It is a laugh: a little girl's laugh, bright as a struck match.
@aurelian: She laughed at everything. At snow. At frogs. At me. How could I forget her laugh?
@aurelian: I kept the pain because I thought it was all I had left of her. But it was never her. She was this.
@narrator: And the old man begins to sing.
@aurelian: "Hush now, little ember, the long night's nearly through. I'll keep the lantern burning, till morning comes for you."
@aurelian: "Rest now, lantern-keeper, the long night's nearly done. Don't look for me in darkness. Look for me in the sun."
@aurelian: That was her part. She was saving it.
!music Dawn
!flash
!sfx Light
@narrator: The cold goes out of the Deep all at once, like a fever breaking. Frost on the walls turns to water, then to light: warm gold light, rising.
@narrator: Out of the Hearthflame they come, one by one: the Wardens, three hundred years of them. Maelis says every name aloud as they pass. She doesn't need the book.
@narrator: Near the last walks a tall young man, no longer made of iron. He nods to Brannoc as he goes. Brannoc nods back.
@narrator: Aurelian rises. For a moment a small shape made of light stands at his side and takes his hand. Then they are gone together, upward.
@narrator: The Hearthflame, with no one left inside it, goes out. No one will ever need it again.
@narrator: Ilsa comes down the last stair, warm again. Together they climb all twenty floors, and at the top the Vaultgate stands open, and sunrise pours down the stair: the first the Deep has ever seen.
!fade
@narrator: Hollowmere, that midsummer. The wells run clear. The lanterns on Chapel Row are lit, not for anyone lost, but so that people can find the door.
@narrator: Tobin lights them now. He is terrible at it. He is getting better.
@narrator: Maelis's true history of the Order runs to four volumes, and the footnotes to six. Pip has planted glowroots in every window box in town. Nobody asked the window boxes.
@narrator: On Lantern Night, Brannoc Ironvein arrives at the dance forty-three years late. Dagna tells him so. Then she dances with him anyway.
@narrator: Elder Marrow, just Marrow now, says he saw his brother's face in that sunrise. Nobody argues with him.
@narrator: Old Hesta sits by the inn fire with Mouser in her lap, and Wren kneels beside her and sings the second verse.
@hesta: That's it. That's the rest of it. My gran swore the song had an answer. Three hundred years it's been waiting.
@ilsa: It's dusk, Wren. Chapel Row?
@wren: Tobin's got it. Let's just watch.
!ending dawn

=== credits
!music Title
@narrator: Thank you for playing Emberdeep.
@narrator: Wherever you are tonight, may someone keep a light on for you, and may you always find the door.
